---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getLocaleFromUrl } from '@/i18n/utils';

const faqItems = [
  {
    question: 'What is the smallest QR code that still scans?',
    answer: 'A QR code smaller than about 1cm × 1cm (0.4 inches) is hard to scan with a phone camera, regardless of the data it contains. Below 0.5cm, most scanners fail. Aim for at least 1.5-2cm for short URLs.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Does print size depend on the scan distance?',
    answer: 'Yes. As a rule of thumb, the QR code width should be at least 1/10th of the scan distance. A code meant to be scanned from 1 meter away should be at least 10cm wide. From 5 meters away: at least 50cm.'
  },
  {
    question: 'What DPI should I print at?',
    answer: 'For print, 300 DPI is standard. Export your QR code as SVG (vector) to print at any size without quality loss. If you must use PNG, export at 600+ DPI for the final print size.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I print a QR code on fabric or curved surfaces?',
    answer: 'Yes, but test first. Curved surfaces (water bottles, mugs) reduce scannability because the code distorts. Use H error correction and test on the actual object. Embroidery on fabric works but requires a high-resolution version and H error correction.'
  },
  {
    question: 'How much white space do I need around the QR code?',
    answer: 'A "quiet zone" of at least 4 modules of white space around all sides. For a 2cm QR code, that means at least 0.5cm of empty white space on each side. Without this margin, scanners often fail.'
  }
];

const locale = getLocaleFromUrl(Astro.url);
---
<Base
  title="QR Code Printing Size Guide: Minimum Sizes & Best Practices"
  description="What's the smallest QR code you can print? How big should it be for poster, business card, or billboard? Print DPI, quiet zones, and distance rules."
  schema="faq"
  faqItems={faqItems}>
  <article class="blog-post">
    <div class="container">
      <header class="blog-header">
        <p class="blog-meta">Reference · 5 min read</p>
        <h1>QR Code Printing Size Guide: Minimum Sizes & Best Practices</h1>
        <p class="blog-lede">A QR code too small won't scan. A QR code too big wastes space. Here's the exact size you need for every common use case — and the print settings that make it work.</p>
      </header>

      <div class="blog-content">
        <p>Print a QR code too small and nobody can scan it. Print it too big and you've wasted half your poster. The sweet spot depends on three things: the size of the data (longer URLs need bigger codes), the scan distance, and the print quality.</p>

        <h2>The 1/10 rule</h2>
        <p>As a rough starting point: the QR code's width should be at least <strong>1/10th of the distance from which it will be scanned</strong>.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Scanned from 10cm away (business card, menu) → 1cm wide minimum</li>
          <li>Scanned from 50cm away (countertop sign) → 5cm wide</li>
          <li>Scanned from 1m away (poster at eye level) → 10cm wide</li>
          <li>Scanned from 5m away (billboard, exhibition stand) → 50cm wide</li>
          <li>Scanned from 10m+ away (building facade, stadium) → 1m+ wide</li>
        </ul>
        <p>These are minimums. Going 1.5-2x larger is always safer.</p>

        <h2>Size chart by use case</h2>
        <table style="width:100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: var(--space-lg) 0;">
          <thead>
            <tr style="background: #f4f4f4;">
              <th style="text-align: left; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">Use case</th>
              <th style="text-align: left; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">Minimum size</th>
              <th style="text-align: left; padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">Recommended</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">Business card</td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">1.5 × 1.5 cm</td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">2 × 2 cm</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">Restaurant menu</td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">2 × 2 cm</td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">3 × 3 cm</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">Product packaging</td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">1.5 × 1.5 cm</td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">2.5 × 2.5 cm</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">Poster (A3)</td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">5 × 5 cm</td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">8 × 8 cm</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">Storefront window</td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">15 × 15 cm</td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">25 × 25 cm</td></tr>
            <tr><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">Billboard</td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">1 × 1 m</td><td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px solid #ddd;">2 × 2 m</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2>The quiet zone</h2>
        <p>A QR code needs empty white space around it — at least <strong>4 modules of white border</strong> on every side. Without this margin, the scanner can't tell where the code ends and the design begins, and scanning fails.</p>
        <p>For a 2cm code, that's about 0.5cm of white space on each side. Many printers and designers don't know this rule and crop the margin. Always include it explicitly in your design.</p>

        <h2>Print quality</h2>
        <p>Three print-quality considerations:</p>
        <h3>1. Use vector format when possible</h3>
        <p>Export the QR code as <strong>SVG</strong> (vector). It scales infinitely without quality loss. Best for print at any size.</p>
        <h3>2. For PNG: export at 600+ DPI</h3>
        <p>If you must use a raster format, export at the final print resolution. For a 2cm × 2cm printed code at 300 DPI, you need a 600×600 pixel PNG minimum. For a 20cm code at 300 DPI, you need 2400×2400px. Generous is better than stingy.</p>
        <h3>3. Don't downscale a small image</h3>
        <p>If you generate the code at 200px and then stretch it to fill a billboard, you'll see every pixel. Always start with high resolution or use SVG.</p>

        <h2>Material considerations</h2>
        <h3>Paper / card</h3>
        <p>Works fine. Avoid very reflective or metallic finishes — they can interfere with scanners.</p>
        <h3>Plastic / laminated</h3>
        <p>Works well. Matte lamination is more scannable than glossy.</p>
        <h3>Glass / acrylic</h3>
        <p>Test on the actual surface. Transparent backgrounds and reflections can confuse scanners.</p>
        <h3>Fabric / clothing</h3>
        <p>Works if printed clearly with H error correction. Embroidery is fine but needs high resolution.</p>
        <h3>Metal / outdoor</h3>
        <p>Use H error correction. Avoid reflective metals — they scatter the scan laser.</p>
        <h3>Curved surfaces (bottles, mugs)</h3>
        <p>Test on the actual curve. A 10% distortion can break a code. Use H error correction and consider shortening the URL.</p>

        <h2>Color considerations</h2>
        <p>The QR code's contrast matters more than the colors. Rules:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Dark on light.</strong> Black on white is the safest. Dark blue on white works. Dark green on white works.</li>
          <li><strong>Avoid light-on-light.</strong> White on yellow fails because there's not enough contrast.</li>
          <li><strong>Light-on-dark can work</strong> (white on black), but some scanners struggle. Test before printing in volume.</li>
          <li><strong>Avoid gradients.</strong> Solid colors only. Gradients confuse scanners.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Testing before mass printing</h2>
        <p>Before printing 1000 business cards or 10,000 product boxes, test the QR code:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Print one sample at the final intended size.</li>
          <li>Test it on at least three different phones (iPhone, Android, multiple OS versions).</li>
          <li>Test under the actual lighting conditions.</li>
          <li>Test at the expected scan distance.</li>
          <li>Test after a few weeks of wear if relevant.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Five minutes of testing can save a re-print run that costs thousands.</p>

        <h2>FAQ</h2>
        {faqItems.map((f) => (
          <details>
            <summary>{f.question}</summary>
            <p>{f.answer}</p>
          </details>
        ))}

        <h2>Try it now</h2>
        <p>Generate a print-ready QR code. Export as SVG for sharp scaling at any size. <a href="/">Generate a QR code →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .blog-post { padding: var(--space-lg) 0; }
  .blog-header { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto var(--space-xl); text-align: center; }
  .blog-meta { color: #777; font-size: 0.875rem; margin-bottom: var(--space-sm); }
  .blog-header h1 { font-size: 2.25rem; line-height: 1.2; }
  .blog-lede { font-size: 1.125rem; color: #555; max-width: 640px; margin: var(--space-md) auto 0; }
  .blog-content { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto; }
  .blog-content h2 { margin-top: var(--space-xl); }
  .blog-content h3 { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }
  .blog-content p, .blog-content li { font-size: 1.0625rem; line-height: 1.7; }
  .blog-content details { margin: var(--space-sm) 0; padding: var(--space-md); border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: 4px; background: #fafafa; }
  .blog-content summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 500; }
</style>